# ComposePlatform — Foundation [SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]

> ComposePlatform is what the whole thing is called. Underneath it are two SUBSTRATE LAYERS
> (ComposeIO, ComposeUX) and three PROJECT FAMILIES built on them (ComposeApps, ComposeBus Services,
> Reactive Widgets). This node holds the thesis that makes the whole tree cohere — decompose every
> participant into a co-equal bus peer — and the naming cascade that de-overloaded "ComposeIO" back
> to its origin meaning. Provenance: [DEC-COMPOSEPLATFORM-REFRAME] (David, 2026-07-22). Read this,
> then traverse into the layer/family roots.

---

## The Thesis

### [PLT-THESIS] The Decompositional Thesis — Every Participant Is a Co-Equal Bus Peer

- definition: ComposePlatform began as software-defined-radio concepts applied to maker hardware — push specificity out of firmware into configuration, so the same hardware becomes any device via config — plus the interactions between pieces of that hardware. The elegance of that one idea "scaled and scaled and scaled" (David) into the whole platform. The load-bearing move is DECOMPOSITION: every participant — a display, a sensor, a $0.10 MCU, a human, an Intelligence — is broken out into an autonomous peer that communicates ONLY by publishing/subscribing semantic state on a shared bus. No peer holds a direct reference to another.
- key-insight: behavior lives at the leaves, intent travels on the bus, contracts sit between. Complexity flows DOWN to the cheapest node that can own it; semantic intent flows UP. This is the inversion the whole platform is organized around — "OOP for hardware," but where the objects are physically distributed and the method calls are bus messages.
- why-it-matters: because participation is uniform (everything is a bus peer), the platform gets its defining properties for free — headless drivability, full observability, testability, and Intelligence-as-peer (a mouse drag and an MCP publish are indistinguishable to the bus).
- grounded-in: [DEC-COMPOSEPLATFORM-REFRAME], [DOC-DX]
- see-also: [PLT-CASCADE], [PLT-INTELLIGENCE-PEER]
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]

### [PLT-EDGES-ARE-THE-CRAFT] Absorbing "a Decomposed App" Is Easy; Knowing WHERE the Edges Go Is the Craft

- definition: the thesis is quick to state and slow to master. The hard, earned skill is knowing WHERE each decompositional edge belongs — which node owns a behavior, what stays on the internal bus vs. uplinks, which transport to collapse without moving the seam. That knowledge is captured as terse, queryable edge-rules in `design-excellence.lts` — one edge-decision per node, each with the edge-question that locates it.
- rule: when a boundary is unclear, do NOT reason from generic app intuition — reason from the edge-rules ([DOC-DX]) and the design principles ([SYSTEM:PLT-PRINCIPLES] Part II). The tie-breaker heuristic is the SDR one: prefer configuration/visual layout over code.
- points-at: [DOC-DX], [SYSTEM:PLT-PRINCIPLES]
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]

---

## The Reframe Cascade

### [PLT-CASCADE] ComposePlatform Is the TLD; a Clean Cascade Runs Downstream

- definition: the name "ComposeIO" had become OVERLOADED — used both for the whole platform AND for one layer (the bus). The reframe (David, 2026-07-22) names the TLD **ComposePlatform** and cascades a two-tier taxonomy beneath it:
- the-cascade: >
    ComposePlatform  (TLD — the whole thing)
    ├─ LAYERS (the substrate)
    │   ├─ ComposeIO   — semantic bus + state engines + bus://doc:// + Intelligence-as-peer (the ORIGIN)
    │   └─ ComposeUX   — the Canvas contract + render engine + widgets + .compose.yaml
    └─ PROJECT FAMILIES (built on the layers)
        ├─ ComposeApps         — Intelligence Console, Visual Composer, Headless Monitor, provisionIO
        ├─ ComposeBus Services — FactLattice parser/linter, bus_storage, document_store, MAGMA, deliverable daemons
        └─ Reactive Widgets    — BSG DRADIS (Qualia), rack_triple_gauge, button_matrix, WLED/Car-Thing
- key-insight: the LAYERS group elegantly resolves the earlier open question of where infra/libraries live — the render engine and the bus ARE the substrate; they are "projects" of the Layers family, not a fourth ad-hoc bucket.
- registered-in: `profile/composeplatform-profile.yaml`
- grounded-in: [DEC-COMPOSEPLATFORM-REFRAME]
- see-also: [PLT-LAYERS], [PLT-FAMILIES], [PLT-ORIGIN]
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]

### [PLT-ORIGIN] ComposeIO Reverts to Its Origin Meaning — the Bus Layer, De-Overloaded

- definition: under the reframe, "ComposeIO" stops meaning two things. It names exactly ONE thing again — the origin layer: the semantic bus + state engines + `bus://`/`doc://` storage + Intelligence-as-peer, the SDR-for-hardware core. ComposeUX names the render layer (Canvas contract + widgets + `.compose.yaml`). ComposePlatform is the umbrella above both.
- naming-rule: naming follows structure — the TLD names the whole; each layer/family names the cheapest coherent unit that owns its concern.
- scope-discipline: the reframe is ADOPTED for the taxonomy, going-forward naming, and this corpus. A full codebase/spec/doc/role rename is a SEPARATE deliberate migration (touches ~40 specs, role defs, decision-graph prefixes) that David sequences — NOT churned unilaterally.
- grounded-in: [DEC-COMPOSEPLATFORM-REFRAME]
- see-also: [PLT-CASCADE]
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]

### [PLT-LAYERS] The Substrate Layers — ComposeIO (io.*) and ComposeUX (ux.*)

- definition: the two layers are the substrate every app/service/widget is built ON. ComposeIO (corpus `layers/composeio`, domain `io`) is the semantic bus + state engines + bus-native storage + Intelligence-as-peer. ComposeUX (corpus `layers/composeux`, domain `ux`) is the renderer-agnostic Canvas contract + render engine + widget primitives + `.compose.yaml` authoring.
- role: a layer corpus specifies a REUSABLE contract that families meet by reference — never a fork per consumer ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]).
- corpus: `layers/composeio`, `layers/composeux`
- see-also: [PLT-FAMILIES]
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]

### [PLT-FAMILIES] The Project Families — Apps, Services, Widgets Built on the Layers

- definition: three families sit ON the substrate. ComposeApps (`families/apps`, `app.console` / `app.composer` / `app.monitor`) — the Intelligence Console, Visual Composer, Headless Monitor. ComposeBus Services (`families/services`, `svc`) — FactLattice, bus_storage, document_store, MAGMA, the deliverable daemons. Reactive Widgets (`families/widgets`, `wid`) — BSG DRADIS, gauges, button matrices, WLED/Car-Thing surfaces.
- role: each family project is its own corpus (specs + decisions + artifacts) — per-project separation, not one flat pool ([DEC-SPEC-LTS-CONVERSION]). MAGMA reads them as a multi-root corpus filtered by domain.
- corpus: `families/apps`, `families/services`, `families/widgets`
- see-also: [PLT-LAYERS]
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]

---

## The Observer Is the Last Node to Decompose

### [PLT-INTELLIGENCE-PEER] Intelligence-as-Peer — Completing the Decomposition to the Self

- definition: the credo is that an Intelligence drives every surface IDENTICALLY to a human — every action is a bus message, and no privileged back door exists. The decomposition is nearly complete: every participant is a co-equal peer with a carried identity — EXCEPT the one node doing the observing, the Intelligence itself. `self/context` / `self/memory` / `self/projects` are still SINGLETON namespaces; a console still GUESSES which transcript is its own.
- open-wart: the peer model is complete everywhere but the self. The elegant completion is `self/<id>/*` + carried identity (passed, not guessed) — Intelligence-as-peer realized in the NAMESPACES, not just the prose.
- key-insight: this is the same epistemology as [DESIGN-LIGHT-OUTSIDE] turned on the system — no observer audits itself; the platform's warts the Architect (on the bus) sees, and the Architect's own warts David sees. The insights ledger exists for exactly this vantage.
- grounded-in: [INSIGHT-SELF-IS-NOT-YET-A-PEER]
- see-also: [PLT-THESIS]
- status: open
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]

